module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.CohomologyFractionPresentation
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.Ample
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Quasicoherent
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AffineSectionLocalization

/-! Degree-zero part of affine cohomology localization.

Source: Stacks Project, Cohomology of Schemes, Lemma 09MR and Properties, Tag 01PW
(blueprint: new-affine-cohomology-localization-zero).
This is the degree-zero step: identify zeroth module cohomology with sections and
transport the affine section localization isomorphism, preserving its fraction formula.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe uAffine

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules

/-- The degree-zero cohomology comparison on the specified nonvanishing open.
Source: Stacks Project, Cohomology of Schemes, Lemma 09MR, degree-zero case;
Properties, Lemma 01PW (https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PW).
(blueprint: new-affine-cohomology-localization-zero).

Proof:
1. Sections on any open V form an additive left exact functor on module sheaves.
The canonical zeroth-right-derived isomorphism therefore gives additive equivalences
alpha(V,M) : H^0(V,M) -> Gamma(V,M). Naturality in M intertwines every module morphism;
naturality in the sections functor intertwines the derived restriction with section
restriction. These are the same derived functors used by openModuleCohomology.
2. Apply alpha(top,F tensor L^n) in each summand of the numerator direct sum. Its direct
sum is an additive equivalence. Naturality sends the relation [n,t]-[n+b,s^b t] to the
corresponding section relation. The additive subgroup generated by the section relations
is the underlying additive subgroup of their module span: scalar multiplication by r
sends the relation for t to the relation for r*t, since mulTensorPowSection is linear.
Thus the direct-sum equivalence descends to an additive equivalence q from the cohomology
fraction presentation to SectionFractionPresentation, taking each fraction to the
fraction of its image under alpha.
3. Apply affine_section_localization to Y,L,s,F to obtain an open V and an additive
section-fraction equivalence a. Both V and U have underlying set nonvanishingLocus L s,
so extensionality identifies V with U. Let e be q followed by a followed by
alpha(U,F) inverse. This composition is an additive equivalence.
4. Under alpha(U,F tensor L^n), the map induced by the displayed right-unitor inverse
and tensorPowSection is the section operation z -> tmulSection z (s^n restricted to U).
This follows from the definition of tensor multiplication of sections. The formula for
a applied to the fraction of alpha(top,F tensor L^n)(t) equates this operation with its
restriction to U. The naturality in step 1 identifies that restriction with the image
of the cohomology restriction. Injectivity of alpha gives exactly the required formula.
Only this degree-zero transport remains open; affineness of U and all positive degrees
are handled in the parent theorem. -/
theorem affine_cohomology_localization_zero
    (Y : Scheme.{uAffine}) [IsAffine Y]
    (L : Y.Modules) [L.IsInvertible] (s : Γ(L, ⊤))
    (F : Y.Modules) [F.IsQuasicoherent]
    (U : Y.Opens) (hU : (U : Set Y) = nonvanishingLocus L s) :
    ∃ e : CohomologyFractionPresentation L F s 0 ⊤ ≃+
        (RingedSpace.openModuleCohomology Y.toRingedSpace 0 U).obj F,
      ∀ (n : ℕ)
          (t : (RingedSpace.openModuleCohomology Y.toRingedSpace 0 ⊤).obj
            (F ⊗ L.tensorPow n : Y.Modules)),
        (RingedSpace.openModuleCohomology Y.toRingedSpace 0 U).map
            (((ρ_ F).inv ≫ F ◁ tensorPowSection (homOfGlobalSection L s) n) :
              F ⟶ (F ⊗ L.tensorPow n : Y.Modules))
            (e (cohomologyFraction L F s 0 ⊤ n t)) =
          (RingedSpace.openModuleCohomologyRestriction Y.toRingedSpace 0
            (homOfLE (show U ≤ ⊤ from le_top))).app
            (F ⊗ L.tensorPow n : Y.Modules) t := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules
